      "target": "../sections/page-001.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "Sero-epidemiological data in the age group of children\naged <4 years in 1999 showed a prevalence rate of 0.16%\ncompared to 5.4% in the same age group in 1983, indicating\nthe interruption of vectorial transmission in this country.\n\nTransmission through blood transfusion is under control\ndue to the 100% coverage of compulsory blood screening in\nthe endemic areas.\n\n(Reported by Programa Nacional de Control de la Enferme-\ndad de Chagas, Ministerio de Salud, Santiago, Informe de la\nComisiôn de Certificacién, November 1999.)\n\nEditorial note. These data rank Chile as the second mem-\nber country of th",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "A page of the Weekly Epidemiological Record of 14 January 2000, in English and French, reporting that Chile interrupted vector-borne and transfusional transmission of Chagas disease in 1999, the second Southern Cone country after Uruguay to reach these goals. It then sets out WHO's position on thiomersal, a mercury-containing vaccine preservative: WHO supports a July 1999 US statement on phasing it out but stresses that children's vaccines containing it should still be used. Since no tested, safe and effective alternative yet exists, WHO will keep recommending vaccines such as DTP, DT, tetanus toxoid and some hepatitis B and Hib vaccines, an approach endorsed by its Expert Committee on Biological Standardization in November 1999.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-11-11"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/10665-231031/blocks/prose-page-002",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Page 2",
      "pageStart": 2,
      "pageEnd": 2,
      "target": "../sections/page-002.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "supports the continued use of current vaccines that protect\nthe lives of millions of children each year.\n\nThe US report mentioned above made recommendations\nto avoid thiomersal-containing hepatitis B vaccine for\ncertain newborns, including the screening of pregnant\nmothers for the blood marker of the liver infection. WHO\nstresses that this is not an option for developing countries,\nowing to practical constraints and high cost. Instead, the\nOrganization is promoting the development of a single-\ndose, affordable presentation of hepatitis B vaccine that\nwill not require a preservative. This devic",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Continues the bilingual Weekly Epidemiological Record note on thiomersal, explaining that screening pregnant women in order to avoid thiomersal-containing hepatitis B vaccine is not workable for developing countries, so WHO is promoting an affordable preservative-free single-dose pouch-and-needle device now in field trials. It summarises what is known about thiomersal toxicity, which is uncertain at low doses and is inferred largely from evidence on methylmercury, and notes that no dose-response curve can be built for vaccine doses. It explains that dietary methylmercury guidelines from WHO and US agencies, ranging from 0.7 to 3.3 µg/kg body weight per week, are therefore used to judge whether vaccine mercury reaches a level of concern, starting from a worst-case low-weight infant.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-11-11"
      }
    },
    {
