      "title": "Page 3",
      "pageStart": 3,
      "pageEnd": 3,
      "target": "../sections/page-003.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "limits for safe total methylmercury exposure are between\n34 wg (according to EPA standards) and 159 yg (according\nto WHO standards). Table 1 shows the exposure that would\ntake place during a typical national immunization\nschedule. During the first 14 weeks of life, an infant gener-\nally receives 3 doses of DTP vaccine or a total of 75 yg\nof ethylmercury. If hepatitis B vaccine is added to the\nimmunization schedule, the maximum exposure to\nethylmercury during the first 14 weeks of life is 112.5 wg.\nAdding Hib vaccine, the total ethylmercury dose reaches\n187.5 ag. This suggests that some infants",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Continues the bilingual note on thiomersal, calculating that an infant on a typical schedule may receive up to 187.5 µg of ethylmercury from DTP, hepatitis B and Hib vaccines in the first 14 weeks, which may be of concern and exceeds some agencies' cut-off levels. A table sets out mercury doses by age and vaccine under two hepatitis B schemes, along with tetanus toxoid doses for women of childbearing age. The note adds that maternal doses do not reach levels of concern, though the effect on the fetal brain is uncertain, and lists knowledge gaps, such as the comparative toxicity of ethylmercury and methylmercury, that make the exact risk hard to identify.",
        "state": "draft",
