      "pageStart": 4,
      "pageEnd": 4,
      "target": "../sections/page-004.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "rently exclude the possibility of subtle neurodevelopmental\nabnormalities from a cumulative exposure of infants to\nthiomersal in vaccines.\n\nDespite a lack of direct evidence of harmful effects of the\npreservative in vaccines, change seems inevitable.\nHowever, the goal of reducing mercury exposure from\nthiomersal in vaccines creates several problems for\nnational immunization programmes. If the condemnation\nof thiomersal were to be too strong, many vital vaccines\nmight be withdrawn from production. If the issue were to\nbe ignored, increasing amounts of thiomersal would be\ninjected into children",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Continues, in parallel English and French, a Weekly Epidemiological Record article on thiomersal in vaccines, noting there is no direct evidence of harm but that cutting mercury exposure poses dilemmas for national immunization programmes. Its bilingual summary judges the theoretical low neurodevelopmental risk to be far outweighed by the dangers of vaccine-preventable disease and contaminated multidose vials, and describes short- and long-term plans by WHO and other agencies to reduce and possibly eliminate the preservative. The page also explains that removing thiomersal would raise contamination risks, strain manufacturers, local producers and the cold chain, while monodose vials are too costly for developing countries and any substitute preservative would take time to license.",
