      "target": "../sections/page-005.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "relicensing, and even with a “fast track” procedure, could\ntake over a year to process.\n\nCombination vaccines have been proposed as a means of\nreducing cold-chain requirements and improving coverage\nby delivering multiple antigens in fewer injections. Also,\nthe amount of preservative needed per vial is the same\nwhether it contains one or many antigens. Thus as more\nantigens are included in a combination vaccine, there will\nbe the need for proportionately less preservative per dose\nof antigen.\n\nNew preservative-free vaccine delivery systems are under\nconstruction. Systems such as powdered jet i",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Concludes the bilingual thiomersal article, noting that relicensing a vaccine with a new preservative could take over a year, that combination vaccines need less preservative per antigen dose, and that preservative-free delivery systems such as powdered jet injection are in development. The page then carries the record's standard notes on geographical designations and on the duty under Article 3 of the International Health Regulations to notify WHO within 24 hours of a first case. It ends with a partly garbled table of cholera and yellow fever notifications received from 7 to 13 January 2000, covering countries in Africa, Asia and the Americas.",
